  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1015 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThe remainder of this week remains fraught with drought and hot \ntemperatures as we see the core of the heat dome slowly retreat from \nthe Smoky Mountains to the southern Great Plains by the weekend. \nTwo minor features worth noting during this time involve the \nremnants of a compact tropical system making landfall along the \nUpper Texas Coast on Tuesday, and a modest intrusion of monsoonal\nmoisture across the Caprock for Thursday and Friday as our flow \naloft turns more southwesterly. Although very unlikely, a few \nmodels drift the tropical remnants with precip into our Rolling \nPlains counties by Friday, with some diurnally-driven storms on \nthe Caprock by Thursday accompanying the monsoonal moisture. \nNeither of these solutions have much support from the multi-day \nensembles, so our forecast officially remains dry. The core of \nthe 596-599 dam high looks to flex its muscles just to our north \nby next weekend which would keep us locked in upper 90s and lower \n100s for highs. NBM and MOS are not capturing this hotter signal \nvery well at the moment and instead show maxT's either leveling\noff or falling, but this will be revisited in later forecasts.",
